A||||7||theogre||Oil Filter Bypass Valve info.||||4||
Z||000000||theogre||04-03-2001||07:47 PM||||I ran across something digging in Carparts.com<P>I knew the oil filter in Fiero (4 cyl and V6) didn't have a bypass valve. What I didn't know is there is one in the block and it is aparantly considered a regular service part. I think it is located under the fitting where the filter mounts but I can't verify it for sure.<P>If this valve has quit or is weak it could account for some peoples oil pressure problems. It is defiantaly something that has to be replaced durring a rebuild. If this vavle is leaking then oil is getting by the filter w/o going thru the media.<P>The part info from carparts is:<P>Oil Filter Bypass Valve, <BR>• Usually in stock and available to ship within 48 hours. AC DELCO 25014006 $4.16 $2.31 <P>That's a standard GM part # so I expect you could get it most anyplace.<P>If anyone has a V6 and/or 4 cyl that you can check the oil filter mount on then please do that and let us know what you find. I know some of you have motors left over from replacements etc.<P>------------------<BR><I><B>Screamin Yellow Zonkers... If it's Screamin and Yellow, I ain't eatin it.</B></I>||||reg||4
Z||000001||Rodrv6||04-03-2001||09:43 PM||||When Pontiac replaced the 4 cylinder in my 84 SE, (recall work), they didn't install the filter bypass valve in the new engine. (According to a GM service bulletin I found, the factory long blocks were shipped without it!!!)  I didn't notice it was missing until I tore the engine down for a rebuild after I had put about 180,000 miles on it.  The amazing thing was I found no problems at all with the bearing surfaces.  A head rebuild and some cylinder work was all it needed at the machine shop.  I guess regular oil changes do a lot of good even if the filter isn't doing much <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||4
Z||000002||Bob Englert||04-03-2001||09:55 PM||||I recently pciked up a bypass valve from NAPA and I hope to replace it when I do my next oil change.  <P>Trying to find the cheap and easy solutions to my low oil pressure problem on my GT at hot idle before I conclude it is an expensive fix.<P>Will let you know what I find...<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phaeton||04-04-2001||02:41 AM||||A weak bypass spring would cause a smaller percentage of oil to be filtered, oil pressure would not be affected.<BR>In normal operation oil is being bypassed continuously.||||reg||12
Z||000004||theogre||04-04-2001||07:28 PM||||I dug a bit more.<P>Actually acording to GM the bypass is closed unless the pressure diferential across the filter exceeds about 8 PSI. This condition should only happen with really cold oil, really dirty oil or filter, colapsed filter when it's blocking it's outlet.<P>It probably won't effect pressure read by the gauge.<P>If I had flaky pressure I'd change the sender first and/or test with another gauge. These senders suck. Mine read perfectly even when the thing was leaking all over the road. One of these days I'm going to get time to see if the newer senders will work with the Fiero gauges and stuff.||||reg||1
Z||000005||StuGood||04-04-2001||08:03 PM||||If you want to see a goofy mechanism, and have some time to kill, try cutting into one of the stock Fiero oil pressure senders (preferably a defective one  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ) and see what's inside.  It's incredibly Rube Goldberg.<P>Sorry if I just slammed some engineer  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>    ||||reg||10
Z||000006||BC-GT||04-04-2001||09:29 PM||||I just replaced my sending unit yesterday, guage was pegged all the time but now it's ok. AC-Delco unit(made in Taiwan though). How much of my Fiero is from another country?<P>------------------<BR>Darryl Martin<BR>1985 GT <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.zing.com/picture/pf251a969c96964ef15691e97e7ac4489/feb64e3a.jpg.orig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||04-05-2001||07:55 PM||||Stu,<P>I did pop the top off the old one.... what a POS. I still have it in my junk pile. I've been keeping an eye out fo a new style one to see how it might be used to replace these.||||reg||1
